#edb/ref 17592186425143 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425145 1069 "q-142567" 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425145 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find ${f}_{X}(3).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |" 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425145 1072 #uuid "7843f1c8-1167-5bc2-84a4-7db8f3081808" 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425145 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.35$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.3$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,0)+f(3,1)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =0.2+0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425141 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425146 1069 "q-142827" 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534547 17592186045417 true] [17592186425146 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\f